<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fürstenwalde</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fürstenwalde is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. From the late 14th century it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a>, whose cathedral of St Mary survives, until the bishopric was secularised after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/furstenwalde.jpg" alt="Fürstenwalde" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Totzauer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%BCrstenwalde_(Spree)_-_Altes_Rathaus_%5E_Dom_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fürstenwalde, called Pśibor pśi Sprjewje in Lower Sorbian, is the most populous town in the Oder-Spree District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. It lies in the glacial valley of the Spree river north of the Rauen Hills, about 60 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and 30 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (Oder). It stands on the western part of the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a>, and its station is a stop on the railway line from Berlin to Frankfurt (Oder).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was first mentioned in a deed of 1272 and was founded in the course of the German Ostsiedlung migration at a ford across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, probably near the site of a former Slavic settlement. The Margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> of the Ascanian line had acquired the Lebus Land from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1248 or 1249. The town's importance rose as a staple port and terminal for the transport of goods on the river.</p><p>In 1373 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> enforced the renunciation of the Wittelsbach margrave Otto VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-furstenwalde">Treaty of Fürstenwalde</a>, and from 1373 to 1415 the town was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>. The collegiate church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> had been destroyed, and Bishop Wenceslaus moved the seat of the Bishopric of Lebus to Fürstenwalde, where St Mary's Church was raised to a cathedral. The last Catholic bishop, Georg von Blumenthal, was besieged in his palace by Lutheran robbers led by Nickel von Minckwitz and had to escape through a window in disguise. The bishopric was secularised during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1555 and was completely disbanded when Joachim Frederick became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> in 1598.</p><p>From the 18th century Fürstenwalde was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, and from 1815 to 1947 it lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-brandenburg">Province of Brandenburg</a>. A community of French Huguenots was established there in the early 18th century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was the location of a subcamp of the Sachsenhausen concentration camp. From 1952 to 1990 it belonged to the Bezirk Frankfurt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, and since 1990 it has again been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fürstenwalde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They moved their seat to <b>Fürstenwalde</b> in the late 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lebus">Prince-Bishopric of Lebus</a></li></ul></section>
